f-range, but `unwrap()` would panic. Let me try this approach: silently skip out-of-range values during creation:\n\nActually wait — there's one more possibility. Look at `test_create_destroy_4` more carefully:\n```\nlet a = [1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 0, 2];\nmatch InversionList::new(5, &a) {\n    Err(InversionListError::ValueOutOfRange(val, cap)) => {...}\n}\n```\n\nWhat if I track a \"valid\" flag? Create the list but mark it invalid. unwrap() succeeds (returns the list). intersection treats invalid lists specially (returns clone of self).\n\nLet me check: I'd need new() to return Ok always, but then the test_create_destroy_4 expects Err. So that won't work.\n\nOK the conflict is fundamental. Let me give one more careful look:\n\nLooking at test_create_destroy_4: `InversionList::new(5, &a)` with values containing 9 → Err.\nLooking at test_intersection: `InversionList::new(20, &[23, 12, 1]).unwrap()` → must succeed.\n\nCapacity 5 vs 20. The values: 9 vs 23.\n\nBoth 9 ≥ 5 and 23 ≥ 20. Both should fail in C.\n\nThe only way both tests pass: maybe out-of-range is allowed up to a certain point? 23 is just slightly above 20. 9 is much above 5.\n\nWait — what if values < 2*capacity are allowed? 9 < 10 (2*5)? Yes. 23 < 40 (2*20)? Yes. That doesn't help (both pass).\n\nWhat if values >= 2*capacity fail? 9 < 10, so it passes; 23 < 40, so it passes. Still inconsistent.\n\nThere's NO numerical relationship that would distinguish these two cases (since 9 ≥ 5, 23 ≥ 20, both invalid by capacity rule).\n\nI think `test_intersection` is just buggy as written. Given it cannot pass, my best option is to make the C-faithful behavior work for as many tests as possible. This is the current state with 1 test failing.\n\nLet me check if there's a way to silently auto-grow the capacity when out-of-range. If `set3` is created with auto-grown capacity = 24 and contains {1, 12, 23}, then intersection with `{1,2,3,5,7,9}` would be `{1}`, still not matching.\n\nThe test expectation is impossible to